#Q1: DIGITAL FORENSIC INVESTIGATION LIFECYCLE

##Definition
The digital forensic investigation lifecycle is a structured process for identifying, preserving, collecting, examining, analysing, and presenting digital evidence in a legally admissible manner.

##The 6 Steps

1. IDENTIFICATION
- Determine what devices or systems may contain evidence.
- Define the scope: which machines, what time range, what data types.
- Money laundering case examples: bank servers, employee laptops, email servers, transaction databases, USB drives found at the scene.
- Do not touch anything yet.

2. PRESERVATION
- Secure the scene. Restrict access.
- Do NOT power off running systems. Volatile memory (RAM) contains running processes, encryption keys, and active network connections. Power off destroys all of this.
- Photograph all devices before touching them. Document: device state (on/off), what is on screen, cables connected.
- Establish chain of custody from this point onward.

3. COLLECTION
- Attach a write-blocker to every storage device before connecting it to the forensic workstation.
- Create a forensic image (bit-by-bit copy of the entire drive including deleted space and slack space).
- Hash the image immediately after creation.
- Work only on the image copy. Never on the original evidence.

```bash
Create forensic image
dd if=/dev/sda of=/mnt/external/image.dd bs=4M

Hash the original drive and the image (both must match)
md5sum /dev/sda
md5sum /mnt/external/image.dd
sha256sum /mnt/external/image.dd > /mnt/external/image.dd.sha256
```

4. EXAMINATION
- Mount the forensic image read-only.
- Tools: Autopsy, FTK, EnCase.
- Actions: recover deleted files using file carving, keyword search, examine metadata, analyse browser history and email.
- Money laundering: search for financial terms ("wire transfer", "offshore", account numbers).

5. ANALYSIS
- Correlate evidence across multiple sources.
- Build a timeline of events.
- Money laundering: trace transaction flow across accounts, match timestamps of financial transfers with communication records.
- Distinguish clearly between facts (what the evidence shows) and inferences (what it suggests).

6. PRESENTATION
- Write the forensic report: case summary, methodology, tools used, findings, conclusions.
- Attach hash values as proof of evidence integrity.
- Format must be acceptable to the court.
- Be prepared to testify as an expert witness and explain technical findings in plain language.

##Lifecycle Diagram (draw this on paper)

```
Identification --> Preservation --> Collection --> Examination --> Analysis --> Presentation
      |                |               |               |              |              |
  Define scope    Secure scene    Write-block +    Recover &      Timeline &    Court report
  List devices    No power off    Image + hash     keyword scan   correlation   + testimony
```

##Key Concepts
- Chain of custody: Continuous log of who handled each piece of evidence, when, and why. Any gap can make evidence inadmissible.
- Write-blocker: Prevents any write operation to the original drive. Mandatory before any connection.
- Bit-by-bit image vs file copy: Forensic image copies every sector including deleted space and slack space. File copy only copies existing files.
- Admissibility: Evidence must be collected using accepted forensic methods and documented to be accepted in court.

##Viva
- Q: Why not power off a running system immediately? A: RAM is volatile. It contains encryption keys, running processes, and network connections that are permanently lost on power off. Capture RAM first using tools like Magnet RAM Capture or WinPmem.
- Q: What happens if chain of custody is broken? A: Defence counsel can challenge the authenticity of the evidence. A judge may rule it inadmissible.
- Q: What is the difference between examination and analysis? A: Examination is finding what is in the evidence (the raw data). Analysis is interpreting what that data means in the context of the case.

