#Q3: PASSWORD CRACKING

##Definition
Password cracking is the process of recovering a plaintext password from its stored hash by generating candidate hashes and comparing them, using automated tools and attack techniques.

##Steps (John the Ripper)
1. Obtain the password hash from the target system.
2. Save it to a text file.
3. Run John the Ripper with the chosen attack mode.
4. View the cracked result.

##Commands
```bash
Save an MD5 hash to a file (example: hash of "password")
echo "5f4dcc3b5aa765d61d8327deb882cf99" > hash.txt

Dictionary attack (fastest when the password is a real word or common variant)
john --wordlist=/usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt --format=Raw-MD5 hash.txt

Brute force attack (tries all character combinations, use when dictionary fails)
john --incremental --format=Raw-MD5 hash.txt

Show cracked passwords
john --show --format=Raw-MD5 hash.txt

For Linux /etc/shadow hashes
john /etc/shadow

For Windows NTLM hashes
john --format=NT hash.txt

Resume an interrupted session
john --restore
```

##Three Attack Types

| Attack Type | Method | Speed | Best Used When |
|---|---|---|---|
| Dictionary attack | Tries every word in a wordlist (rockyou.txt has 14M entries) | Fast | Password is a common word, name, or simple variation |
| Rainbow table attack | Looks up precomputed hash-to-password mappings | Very fast (lookup only, no computation) | Hash is unsalted and target is in the precomputed table |
| Brute force attack | Tries every possible character combination | Very slow | Dictionary and rainbow both failed; password length is known to be short |

##Why Rainbow Tables Fail Against Salted Hashes
- A salt is a random string added to the password before hashing.
- `hash(password + salt)` produces a unique output per user even if two users have identical passwords.
- Rainbow tables are precomputed for `hash(password)` only. The salt invalidates all precomputed entries.
- Modern systems (bcrypt, scrypt, Argon2) use salting by default and are designed to be slow to compute.

##Key Concepts
- Hash: A one-way function. You cannot reverse a hash mathematically. Cracking works by generating hashes of candidates and comparing outputs.
- rockyou.txt: A leaked real-world password list with 14 million entries. The standard dictionary for John and Hashcat.
- GPU cracking: Hashcat uses GPU acceleration. It cracks MD5 hashes billions of times faster than John the Ripper on CPU.
- Salting: Renders rainbow tables useless and makes dictionary attacks significantly harder.

##Viva
- Q: What is the difference between dictionary attack and brute force? A: Dictionary attack tries pre-selected words and known variations. Brute force tries every possible character combination. Dictionary is faster but fails against random passwords. Brute force is exhaustive but slow beyond 8 characters.
- Q: Why is MD5 weak for password storage? A: MD5 is designed to be fast. A modern GPU can compute billions of MD5 hashes per second. Use bcrypt or Argon2, which are deliberately slow and include salt by design.
- Q: Is password cracking always illegal? A: No. It is legal and necessary during authorised penetration testing, forensic investigations with a court order, and recovering your own passwords.

