#Q4: SEARCH AND SEIZURE PROCEDURE

##Definition
Search and seizure in digital forensics is the legally authorised process of locating, documenting, and collecting electronic evidence without altering or contaminating it.

##Steps

Phase 1: Pre-Search Preparation
- Obtain a valid search warrant specifying location, devices, and scope.
- Assemble forensic toolkit: write-blockers, imaging drives, anti-static evidence bags, labels, camera, chain of custody forms.
- Brief the team on scope, legal boundaries, and target evidence types.
- Tax evasion / money laundering: brief team to look for financial spreadsheets, accounting software (Tally, QuickBooks), PDF invoices, email archives, browser history showing online banking.

Phase 2: Securing the Scene
- Seal the perimeter. No unauthorised person enters or leaves.
- Do NOT power off any running computer. Volatile data will be lost.
- Photograph the entire room before touching anything. Capture: screen contents, device positions, cables, physical condition of each device.
- Record which devices are on and which are off. Enter this in the scene log.

Phase 3: Documenting Devices
- Label every device with a unique evidence number (E001, E002, etc.).
- Record: device type, brand, model, serial number, physical condition, exact location in the room.
- For mobile phones: enable airplane mode immediately to prevent remote wipe. Then place in a Faraday bag to block all wireless signals.
- Do not connect any device to a USB port or network before attaching a write-blocker.

Phase 4: Evidence Acquisition
- Attach hardware write-blocker.
- Create forensic image with dd.
- Hash the image immediately.
- Record hash value in the chain of custody form.
- Package the original device in an anti-static evidence bag. Seal and sign it.

```bash
Forensic imaging
dd if=/dev/sda of=/mnt/backup/evidence_E001.dd bs=4M conv=noerror,sync

Hash verification (run both)
md5sum /mnt/backup/evidence_E001.dd
sha256sum /mnt/backup/evidence_E001.dd

Save hashes to file
md5sum /mnt/backup/evidence_E001.dd > evidence_E001.md5
sha256sum /mnt/backup/evidence_E001.dd > evidence_E001.sha256
```

Phase 5: Chain of Custody
Every handover of evidence must be recorded:
- Evidence number, item description
- Name and designation of person releasing custody
- Name and designation of person receiving custody
- Date, time, location of transfer
- Reason for transfer
- Signature of both parties

Phase 6: Tax Evasion / Money Laundering Specific
- Priority targets: financial spreadsheets, Tally/QuickBooks database files, PDF invoices, email archives, browser history (online banking).
- Check for: hidden partitions, encrypted containers (VeraCrypt volumes appear as featureless large files), cloud sync folders (Google Drive, Dropbox).
- Examine: deleted files, recycle bin, Windows shadow copies (VSS), recent documents list, USB device connection history (registry).
- Goal: recover transaction logs, match timestamps of financial transfers with communication records.

##Chain of Custody Table (draw this on paper)

| Field | Entry |
|---|---|
| Evidence No. | E001 |
| Item Description | Seagate HDD, 1TB, Serial: ST1000DM003 |
| Collected by | [Name, Designation] |
| Collection date/time | 31-Jul-2026 / 09:45 AM |
| Location collected from | Office of accused, Desk 3, 2nd floor |
| Current location | Forensic lab, Evidence locker 4 |
| Hash (MD5) | 5f4dcc3b5aa765d61d8327deb882cf99 |
| Hash (SHA256) | [value] |
| Remarks | Device was powered off at time of collection |

##Key Concepts
- Anti-static bag: Prevents electrostatic discharge from damaging the storage device and corrupting evidence.
- Faraday bag: Blocks all radio signals (WiFi, 4G, Bluetooth). Used for phones to prevent remote wipe commands.
- Write-blocker: Allows read access only. Any write attempt is blocked at the hardware level.
- Admissibility: Evidence is admissible if it is authentic, unaltered, lawfully collected, and properly documented throughout.

##Viva
- Q: What do you do if you find an encrypted hard drive? A: Do not attempt to decrypt at the scene. Image it as-is. Look for the key in RAM if the system is running. Seek legal compulsion on the suspect to provide the decryption key.
- Q: Why use both MD5 and SHA256? A: MD5 is fast and widely used for quick comparison. SHA256 is cryptographically stronger. Using both ensures integrity even if one algorithm is later challenged in court.
- Q: What is the risk of powering off a running laptop before imaging? A: Full-disk encryption (BitLocker, VeraCrypt) may be active. Powering off locks the drive. The image becomes unreadable without the key, which was in RAM.

